# FACTORY (the author, 2026-10-05): `@: h f(a)` receives a factory's result; this row wrote `h: f a` until then.
# A method that stands above every declaration of a name has no binding of
# it: a head of that name is an unknown head there, whatever the unit
# declares later. paren and colon stand above the store of h2. Their
# statements `h2(5 6)` and `h2: 7 8` are one form in two spellings: each
# defines a Structure of the method with the written contents, and neither
# calls the callable the root holds. The callable notes its actuals: the
# trace holds the root's own call alone, 12. Entry 7.
int: trace 0

fn: note (int: v) int
    node\trace: node\trace * 100 + v
    return: 0
end: note

fn: seen () int
    return: node\trace
end: seen

fn: paren () int
    h2(5 6)
    return: 0
end: paren

fn: colon () int
    h2: 7 8
    return: 0
end: colon

fn: make2 (int: n) fn: (int: x; int: y) int
    fn: f2 (int: x; int: y) int
        return: n + note(x * 10 + y)
    return: f2

@: h2 make2(100)
int: a paren()
int: b colon()
int: c h2(1 2)
int: ok 7
if: seen() != 12
    ok: 81
if: c != 100
    ok: 82
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
